<BaseLayout title="About" description="Learn about MushroomScope, its editorial process, organizational byline, sources, limitations, corrections, and safety-first mission."><div class="shell py-8"><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'About'}]} /></div><article class="shell max-w-4xl py-12"><p class="eyebrow">Our mission</p><h1 class="mt-4 font-serif text-5xl sm:text-6xl">A clearer view of the fungal world</h1><div class="prose mt-8"><p>MushroomScope is an independent English-language knowledge project for mushroom enthusiasts, growers, nature lovers, cooks, and curious readers worldwide. The site is built around practical questions: what can be observed, what can be cooked safely, what can be grown responsibly, and what evidence actually supports a health claim.</p><h2>How we work</h2><p>Our goal is to publish approachable, structured references that distinguish established knowledge from emerging evidence. We compare taxonomic databases, institutional food-safety guidance, university extension material, peer-reviewed research, and specialist mycological literature. References, review dates, image notes, and revision summaries are displayed so readers can inspect the basis of an article.</p><p>Published pages must satisfy category-specific checks before they appear on the site. Species pages require taxonomy and identification context; health pages require evidence labels and medical disclaimers; recipes require structured ingredients and instructions; growing pages require method, substrate, condition, timeline, and contamination fields.</p><h2>What our byline means</h2><p>“MushroomScope Editorial Team” is an organizational byline for material researched and maintained by this project. It is not a claim that an article has been examined by a named mycologist, clinician, or dietitian. When qualified external review is obtained, the reviewer’s name, relevant credentials, review scope, and date will be disclosed on the article.</p><h2>Topic hubs</h2><p>Readers can follow complete learning paths through the <a href="/hubs/mushroom-identification-safety/">mushroom identification safety hub</a>, <a href="/hubs/mushroom-nutrition-evidence/">mushroom nutrition evidence hub</a>, <a href="/hubs/beginner-mushroom-growing/">beginner mushroom growing hub</a>, <a href="/hubs/easy-mushroom-recipes/">easy mushroom recipes hub</a>, and <a href="/hubs/oyster-mushroom-guide/">oyster mushroom topic hub</a>.</p><h2>Safety and limitations</h2><p>Online content cannot confirm the identity or edibility of a wild mushroom. Identification pages are educational and require local, in-person expert confirmation before any consumption decision. Health pages do not provide medical diagnosis or treatment. We do not use generated mushroom images as identification evidence.</p><h2>Corrections and editorial standards</h2><p>Articles are designed to cite credible sources, disclose uncertainty, and avoid medical or identification certainty where it is not justified. Readers can report a factual concern through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Read our full <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> and <a href="/disclaimer/">safety disclaimer</a>.</p><h2>Explore MushroomScope</h2><p>Start with the <a href="/mushrooms/">mushroom encyclopedia</a>, use the <a href="/search/">knowledge search</a>, or explore the developing sections for <a href="/identification/">identification</a>, <a href="/growing/">cultivation</a>, <a href="/health/">health research</a>, and <a href="/recipes/">cooking</a>.</p></div></article></BaseLayout>
